NAS with real parity is a prerequisite astro-serving assumes but we don't have

cld-strand-astro-serving.md scopes redundancy tightly and correctly: the ONLY
thing that cannot be lost is the captured frames; everything downstream is
reproducible while the pipeline is in git. It then assumes "raw archive lives on
the NAS with real parity" — which does not exist yet. Today bigstore is one
SMART-blind copy (Seagate Expansion enclosure blocks ATA pass-through), so the
raw archive is currently the LEAST protected part of the chain, not the most.

This is the concrete form of "redundancy not capacity" — the goal is to stop
being at one copy. It also gates the other two astro-serving hardware items:
the NAS decision determines the storage topology, which determines how thin tin
can be. Sequence: NAS parity first, then tin, then nit's RAM ceiling.
